(function(){'use strict';const{register,table,diagram}=window.AcademyContent;
register({id:55,centralQuestion:'Should the next unit of capacity be a larger machine or another independent replica?',objective:'Choose vertical versus horizontal scaling from bottlenecks, state, partitionability, startup delay, failure domains and economics; configure meaningful HPA signals.',sections:[
{id:'scale',title:'Scaling changes the location of contention',body:['Vertical scaling adds resources to an instance: more CPU, memory or a larger accelerator. It can make a model fit and avoid distributed communication, but reaches hardware limits and concentrates failure. Horizontal scaling adds instances. It improves parallel request capacity when work is independent, but duplicates weights, increases coordination and can overload shared databases or artifact stores.','A model too large for one device cannot be fixed by adding independent full-model replicas that also cannot fit. It needs a larger-memory device, reduced state/precision, or model partitioning. Data parallel serving replicates a model for independent requests; tensor or pipeline parallelism partitions one model and introduces communication and scheduling complexity.','Choose the scaling unit from the actual limiting resource. More API replicas do little when a single vector database is saturated. More GPUs do little when input preprocessing is serial. Measure sustainable per-instance throughput at the SLO before projecting fleet capacity.'],table:table('Scale-up versus scale-out',['Decision','Vertical','Horizontal'],[['Model fit','Can increase memory per instance','Full replicas still must fit'],['Request parallelism','Limited by one instance','Independent replicas add capacity'],['Failure','Larger single loss','Can spread across domains'],['Cost','Hardware steps and idle headroom','Copies, routing and shared dependencies'],['State','Simpler local state','External state/partitioning needed']]),check:{question:'Why do ten small GPUs not automatically serve a model that fits on none of them?',answer:'Independent replicas each need the full model; partitioning requires an explicitly supported distributed execution design.'}},
{id:'autoscale',title:'Autoscaling reacts to measurements after time has passed',body:['Horizontal Pod Autoscaling adjusts replica targets from observed resource or custom metrics. CPU utilization typically depends on configured resource requests, so an unrealistic request distorts the signal. GPU inference may need queue age, active sequences, tokens per second, memory pressure or device utilization rather than CPU alone.','Scaling has delay: metric collection, controller decision, node provisioning, scheduling, image/model load and warmup. A queue can violate its deadline before new capacity becomes ready. Keep minimum warm capacity and headroom for the expected burst, use leading demand signals and cap admission during the gap.','Stabilization windows and separate scale-up/down behavior prevent oscillation. Rapid scale-down can evict warm caches and create another cold-start spike; indefinite overprovisioning wastes cost. Declare maximum replicas and budget limits, but also define what happens when that maximum is reached.'],failure:'An HPA targeting 60% CPU on a GPU-bound service can remain idle while the request queue grows. The metric must represent the workload bottleneck.',check:{question:'What should a service do while new replicas are still loading?',answer:'Use warm headroom, bounded queues, admission control or a validated fallback; scaling alone cannot rescue already-expired requests.'}},
{id:'economics',title:'Validate scale efficiency under a real failure',body:['If one replica serves 50 QPS at the SLO, four replicas do not necessarily serve 200 QPS. Routing imbalance, shared stores, network limits and burst shape can reduce efficiency. Test the fleet with one replica unavailable and with long requests, not only evenly distributed short inputs.','Track useful throughput per added cost and the marginal bottleneck after each step. A larger device may reduce copies and communication; smaller replicas may improve elasticity and fault isolation. Reservations and minimum capacity affect cost even when traffic is low.','An architecture decision should specify the scaling trigger, startup time, maximum, state dependency, N−1 capacity and overload behavior. This makes the trade-off reviewable and exposes whether the claimed elasticity depends on an unrealistic instant start.'],example:'At 120 QPS with 50 QPS sustainable per replica, three replicas have only 30 QPS headroom and cannot survive one loss. Four replicas preserve 150 QPS after one fails, before accounting for routing imbalance.',check:{question:'Why size for N−1 capacity?',answer:'A fleet that meets demand only when every replica is healthy cannot maintain the same SLO during a single planned or unplanned loss.'}}
],glossary:[['Vertical scaling','Increasing resources of an instance.'],['Horizontal scaling','Adding parallel instances.'],['Data parallel serving','Replicating the full model for independent requests.'],['Tensor parallelism','Partitioning tensor computation across devices.'],['HPA','Controller adjusting Pod replica count from metrics.'],['Stabilization window','Period smoothing scaling decisions to reduce oscillation.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Size a scaling policy',brief:'Peak demand is 120 QPS; each replica sustains 50 QPS; warmup takes three minutes; a burst doubles traffic within 30 seconds.',parts:['Compute minimum capacity with one-replica loss.','Choose scaling signals and warm headroom.','Define maximum capacity and overload behavior.','Compare one large GPU with smaller replicas.'],solution:'Four replicas are the simple N−1 minimum for 120 QPS, subject to measured fleet efficiency. A three-minute cold start cannot absorb a 30-second doubling: reserve burst headroom, predictive scaling or bounded degradation. Use queue age/in-flight/GPU signals; cap queues and reject with retry guidance. Compare full cost, fit, p99, state-store capacity and failure domains.'},sources:{core:['kubernetesHPA','azureWellArchitectedAI'],deep:['kubernetesDeploy']},quiz:[
{concept:'Scaling unit',prompt:'The model needs 40 GB but each new replica has 16 GB. What is missing?',answer:0,options:[['A fit strategy such as larger memory, quantization or explicit partitioning.','Replication alone does not pool memory.'],['Only more identical full replicas.','Each still fails to fit.'],['A different HTTP status.','It does not add memory.'],['A smaller dashboard.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'HPA signal',prompt:'GPU and queue are saturated while CPU is low. Which scaling signal is preferable?',answer:1,options:[['CPU alone.','It misses this bottleneck.'],['Queue age/in-flight plus relevant accelerator signals.','They reflect constrained work.'],['Training epoch count.','It is not serving demand.'],['Random replica changes.','They are not evidence-driven.']]},
{concept:'N−1 capacity',prompt:'Each replica handles 50 QPS; demand is 120 QPS. Minimum replicas to retain capacity after one loss?',answer:2,options:[['Two.','Only 50 QPS survives one loss.'],['Three.','Only 100 QPS survives.'],['Four.','Three survivors provide 150 QPS before overhead.'],['One.','It cannot meet demand even when healthy.']]},
{concept:'Scaling delay',prompt:'Warmup is four minutes, burst tolerance is 20 seconds. What must supplement reactive scaling?',answer:3,options:[['Longer unbounded queues.','Requests expire and consume memory.'],['Ignoring the burst.','The SLO fails.'],['Deleting readiness checks.','Unloaded instances receive traffic.'],['Warm reserve/predictive capacity and bounded admission or fallback.','Capacity must exist before deadlines expire.']]},
{concept:'Shared bottleneck',prompt:'Doubling API replicas leaves throughput unchanged and database latency rises. What follows?',answer:0,options:[['The shared state layer is now limiting and needs diagnosis.','Front-end scale amplified its demand.'],['Add unlimited API replicas.','That increases contention.'],['Remove database authentication.','Unsafe and not a capacity solution.'],['Change the loss function.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Scale-down',prompt:'Aggressive scale-down causes repeated cold starts. What policy helps?',answer:1,options:[['Scale to zero after every request.','It maximizes cold starts.'],['Stabilization and a justified warm minimum.','It reduces oscillation and startup penalties.'],['Disable monitoring.','Then behavior is invisible.'],['Always double model size.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Scaling economics',prompt:'Four replicas deliver only 2.5× one-replica throughput. What should planning use?',answer:2,options:[['An assumed perfect 4×.','Measurements contradict it.'],['Only hardware marketing throughput.','It omits system constraints.'],['Measured fleet throughput at SLO and its marginal cost.','This reflects actual efficiency.'],['Training accuracy as QPS.','Units and purpose differ.']]}
]});
register({id:56,centralQuestion:'When work arrives faster than it can finish, which requests should wait and which should be refused?',objective:'Design routing, load balancing, queues, deadlines, backpressure, fairness and admission control for bursty heterogeneous inference.',sections:[
{id:'routing',title:'Equal request counts do not imply equal work',body:['Round-robin routing distributes request counts, but a 100-token query and a 20,000-token query impose different compute and memory. Least-connections routing estimates load from active work but can still miss request size. Queue-aware or cost-aware routing can improve balance when its measurements are timely and stable.','Session affinity preserves caches but can create hot replicas. Tenant-aware routing may isolate noisy neighbors, while locality reduces network transfer. Every optimization needs a fallback when a preferred instance fails. Avoid routing feedback so aggressive that every request chases the same momentarily idle replica.','Define the unit of admission: requests, tokens, estimated GPU memory, jobs or tenant budget. A global QPS limit alone may not protect a server from a few enormous prompts. Validate input length and output limits before expensive work begins.'],diagram:diagram('flow','Bounded demand handling',['Gateway + tenant budget','Size/deadline validation','Bounded queue/router','Ready replicas','Result or explicit overload'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[1,4],[2,4]],'Work exceeding capacity is rejected or deferred explicitly; it does not accumulate without a deadline.'),check:{question:'Why can round-robin overload one replica despite equal request counts?',answer:'Requests differ in duration, context length, memory and tool use; equal counts are not equal resource demand.'}},
{id:'queue',title:'A queue stores waiting, not capacity',body:['Queues absorb short bursts when average service capacity eventually exceeds arrival. If sustained arrival exceeds completion, backlog grows until deadlines, memory or storage fail. Queue age is often more actionable than depth because it connects directly to freshness and latency promises.','Set maximum length, maximum residence time, priority/fairness policy and cancellation behavior. A stale inference request may no longer have business value; remove it before compute. A durable batch job may remain useful for hours and can be deferred instead of rejected. Separate interactive and bulk queues so large background jobs cannot block urgent work.','Backpressure communicates inability to accept more work to producers. For HTTP, use a documented overload response and retry guidance; for streams/queues, pause consumption or bound producer rate. Backpressure must propagate to the origin instead of moving an unbounded buffer into the next service.'],table:table('Demand policy',['Work type','Useful response to overload','Risk'],[['Interactive prediction','Reject/fallback within deadline','Unbounded waiting breaks latency'],['Durable report job','Queue with job ID and completion SLA','Duplicate or forgotten work'],['Safety-critical action','Safe abstention/human workflow','Unsafe guessed output'],['Bulk embeddings','Throttle/defer','Starving interactive traffic']]),failure:'Increasing queue size after every overload can make dashboards look less error-prone while users wait longer and expired work wastes compute. Track completed useful work, not accepted requests alone.',check:{question:'When does a queue help?',answer:'When it absorbs a bounded burst that can drain within the work’s deadline and available future capacity.'}},
{id:'fairness',title:'Admission is also a fairness and cost decision',body:['One tenant can consume all concurrency with long jobs. Per-tenant quotas, weighted fair scheduling and separate priority classes reserve service for others. Priority must not cause indefinite starvation: include aging or explicit lower-tier guarantees. Rate limits regulate arrivals; concurrency limits bound live resource occupation.','Retries require the same original deadline and action identity. A client that retries immediately after a 429 or 503 can amplify overload. Return useful retry timing where possible, add jitter, and cap attempts. Do not retry a permanently invalid request.','Load tests should include skewed input sizes, one abusive tenant, burst arrivals, dependency slowdown and cancellation. Measure queue-age distribution, rejection rate, fairness, useful throughput and cost per completed task. This reveals whether routing merely moves pressure between replicas.'],example:'A tenant submits 200 long report jobs while another needs a 300 ms classification. Separate bulk and interactive capacity; otherwise a FIFO queue makes the short request wait behind hours of valid but lower-urgency work.',check:{question:'Why use both rate and concurrency limits?',answer:'Rate bounds arrivals over time; concurrency bounds simultaneously occupied memory and execution slots, especially for long requests.'}}
],glossary:[['Backpressure','Signal or control that slows upstream producers when capacity is constrained.'],['Admission control','Decision to accept work based on bounded resources and deadlines.'],['Queue age','Elapsed waiting time of queued work.'],['Head-of-line blocking','Short or ready work waiting behind a slow earlier item.'],['Weighted fair scheduling','Allocation of service among classes or tenants by explicit shares.'],['Load shedding','Rejecting lower-value/excess work to preserve service for admitted work.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Protect interactive inference from bulk jobs',brief:'A shared service processes short classifications and hour-long report batches. One tenant can submit unlimited jobs.',parts:['Design queues and routing policies.','Set rate, concurrency, length and deadline limits.','Define overload responses and retry behavior.','Test fairness and cancellation under a tenfold burst.'],solution:'Separate interactive/bulk queues and reserve interactive capacity. Enforce tenant token/job budgets, bounded concurrency, maximum queue age and input/output length. Return job IDs for durable work, 429/503 with bounded jittered retry guidance for excess synchronous load, and cancel expired work. Verify small tenants retain service and that canceled jobs release resource slots.'},sources:{core:['azureWellArchitectedAI','kubernetesService'],deep:['fastapi','otel']},quiz:[
{concept:'Load balancing',prompt:'Equal request counts produce unequal GPU memory use. Which information would improve routing?',answer:2,options:[['Only request ID spelling.','It has no cost meaning.'],['Only model accuracy.','It does not estimate live load.'],['Input/output size, active work and queue/memory pressure.','These reflect heterogeneous demand.'],['Only the number of API routes.','Not the per-request burden.']]},
{concept:'Queue stability',prompt:'Arrival remains 120 jobs/s while capacity is 100 jobs/s. What does a larger queue achieve?',answer:0,options:[['It delays failure while backlog continues growing.','Sustained demand still exceeds service.'],['It creates 20 jobs/s of compute.','Storage is not capacity.'],['It guarantees bounded latency.','Waiting increases.'],['It eliminates need for admission.','Admission remains necessary.']]},
{concept:'Deadline',prompt:'A queued fraud decision is already past its action deadline. What is normally appropriate?',answer:1,options:[['Compute it at highest priority without checking value.','It may no longer be actionable.'],['Cancel or route to a defined late-result workflow.','Avoid wasting capacity on expired work.'],['Remove all future deadlines.','That worsens overload.'],['Pretend it completed on time.','That corrupts evidence.']]},
{concept:'Fairness',prompt:'One tenant’s long jobs occupy all slots. Which control directly addresses this?',answer:3,options:[['A prettier status page.','It does not allocate capacity.'],['Only a global daily request count.','A burst can still monopolize slots.'],['A higher model temperature.','Unrelated.'],['Per-tenant concurrency quotas and fair scheduling.','They bound monopolization.']]},
{concept:'Backpressure',prompt:'A service rejects work but its caller buffers it without limit. Has backpressure reached the source?',answer:0,options:[['No; the backlog was merely moved upstream.','The producer must slow or shed work.'],['Yes, all buffers are safe.','Unbounded memory can fail.'],['Yes, if HTTP uses JSON.','Format is irrelevant.'],['No queue is ever useful.','Bounded queues are useful.']]},
{concept:'Head-of-line blocking',prompt:'A 20 ms task waits behind a two-hour batch. What design reduces this?',answer:2,options:[['One unlimited FIFO for all work.','That preserves the blockage.'],['Longer synchronous timeouts.','They only prolong waiting.'],['Separate queues/classes with reserved capacity.','Different urgency gets explicit service.'],['Deleting the short task silently.','That violates the contract.']]},
{concept:'Overload retry',prompt:'Clients instantly retry every overload response five times. What should the contract add?',answer:1,options:[['Unlimited attempts.','They amplify demand.'],['Retry guidance, jitter, attempt caps and original deadlines.','These bound retry pressure.'],['Retry validation failures too.','Permanent errors will not heal.'],['No error codes.','Clients need actionable status.']]}
]});
register({id:57,centralQuestion:'Can cached or batched work remain the same decision after the user, permissions, and data change?',objective:'Choose batching and caching policies with explicit compatibility, latency, keying, invalidation, tenancy and freshness semantics.',sections:[
{id:'batch',title:'Batching trades waiting for efficient execution',body:['A fixed batch groups work before execution. Dynamic batching waits for a short window or a size limit to combine compatible live requests. Larger batches can improve device utilization and amortize launch overhead, but add queue delay and memory. Variable-length sequences force padding unless bucketed, so one long request can waste compute for many short ones.','Continuous batching for generation admits new sequences as others finish rather than waiting for an entire fixed group. It can improve utilization under variable output lengths, but KV-cache capacity and scheduling fairness become central. Track first-token and total latency as well as tokens per second.','Set batch size, wait window, shape compatibility and deadline-aware admission together. A 50 ms batching window cannot serve a 40 ms end-to-end deadline. Benchmark the actual arrival distribution; at low traffic, every request may pay the full window for little efficiency gain.'],table:table('Efficiency technique',['Benefit','Semantic/operational condition'],[['Dynamic batching','Amortized compute overhead','Compatible shapes and bounded waiting'],['Length bucketing','Less padding','Enough traffic per bucket'],['Embedding cache','Avoid repeated encoding','Text + model + preprocessing version key'],['Response cache','Avoid full generation','Identity, permission, context and freshness equivalence'],['Local artifact cache','Faster startup','Immutable digest and integrity verification']]),check:{question:'Why can length bucketing improve throughput?',answer:'It reduces padding work by grouping inputs with similar sequence lengths, though more buckets can increase waiting at low traffic.'}},
{id:'cache',title:'A cache key is a claim of semantic equivalence',body:['An embedding cache key should include normalized text, embedding-model version and preprocessing policy. A query embedding from a different model may have the same vector dimension but a different space. A response cache often needs user/tenant authority, prompt/model/index versions, tool state, locale and relevant request parameters. Hashing only the user’s sentence can leak another tenant’s answer.','Exact caching returns a result for an identical key. Semantic caching reuses a nearby query, adding the risk that similar wording hides different intent, date, entity or permission. Validate similarity thresholds on task-specific error costs and use stricter policies for consequential answers.','TTL is a freshness bound, not proof of validity. Invalidate or version keys when source documents, policies or permissions change. Authorization should be checked at retrieval of cached content; a user whose access was revoked must not retain access merely because a response was generated yesterday.'],failure:'A shared response cache keyed only by question text can return private payroll data across tenants. Cache design must preserve the authorization context of the original computation.',check:{question:'Why is equal embedding dimension insufficient for cache reuse after a model change?',answer:'Coordinates represent a model-specific space; equal length does not make vectors semantically compatible.'}},
{id:'locality',title:'Count cache misses, stale hits, and useful savings',body:['Data locality reduces repeated network transfer by placing computation near data or caching immutable artifacts on nodes. It can shorten cold starts, but a fleet rollout can still stampede a shared source. Coordinate downloads, use content-addressed caches and bound simultaneous warmup.','Measure hit rate alongside correctness, staleness, authorization failures and cost per successful task. A high hit rate from serving old answers is not an improvement. Cache misses during a popular-key expiry can overwhelm the backend; single-flight request coalescing and staggered expiry can reduce duplicate regeneration.','Batching and caching interact. A cache removes easy repeated requests, leaving a more heterogeneous or expensive miss workload. Rebenchmark the remaining path and autoscaling signals. A cache outage should degrade predictably through bounded backend admission rather than releasing every request at once.'],example:'An HR assistant caches “What is my leave balance?” by text. Every employee asks the same words, but the answer depends on identity and current records. This query is unsuitable for a shared text-only cache; use authorized per-user data or compute it afresh.',check:{question:'Why might backend average cost rise after a successful cache is added?',answer:'Cheap repetitive requests become hits, so the remaining misses are disproportionately expensive; evaluate total system cost and the changed miss distribution.'}}
],glossary:[['Dynamic batching','Runtime grouping of compatible requests within a bounded wait.'],['Padding','Extra tokens/elements used to align shapes in a batch.'],['Semantic cache','Reuse based on meaning similarity rather than exact key identity.'],['TTL','Time-to-live limiting how long a cached item is eligible.'],['Cache stampede','Many simultaneous misses causing duplicate expensive regeneration.'],['Single-flight','Coalescing concurrent identical work into one execution.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Design safe caching for a company assistant',brief:'The assistant answers public policy questions and employee-specific payroll questions. Policy documents update weekly.',parts:['Define separate caching eligibility and keys.','Set invalidation and authorization checks.','Choose a batching window under a 500 ms first-token budget.','Plan cache-outage and stampede tests.'],solution:'Cache public policy answers only with prompt/model/index and policy versions, plus locale and relevant parameters; validate citations and freshness. Avoid shared caching for payroll; any per-user cache must recheck authorization and freshness. Benchmark a small batching window within the full latency budget. Coalesce identical misses, stagger expiry and bound backend admission on cache failure.'},sources:{core:['azureWellArchitectedAI','hfTokenizers'],deep:['ragPaper','kubernetesHPA']},quiz:[
{concept:'Cache authorization',prompt:'Two tenants ask the same payroll question and share a text-only response key. What is the main risk?',answer:0,options:[['Cross-tenant data disclosure.','The key omits authority and identity.'],['Only lower GPU utilization.','Security is the central failure.'],['Guaranteed better accuracy.','The answer may belong to another tenant.'],['No risk if the text matches.','Same text does not imply same entitlement.']]},
{concept:'Embedding version',prompt:'An embedding model changes but vector dimensions stay equal. Can old cached query vectors be used blindly?',answer:2,options:[['Yes, dimension defines the space.','Coordinates are model-specific.'],['Yes, if stored in JSON.','Serialization does not align spaces.'],['No; version the model/preprocessing and rebuild compatible vectors.','Equal dimension is insufficient.'],['No embeddings can be cached.','Versioned caching is valid.']]},
{concept:'Batch deadline',prompt:'A batcher waits 60 ms for a service with a 50 ms total deadline. What is wrong?',answer:1,options:[['Nothing if throughput rises.','The deadline is already consumed.'],['The wait policy alone can violate the user deadline.','Batching must fit the full budget.'],['The GPU must be too small.','The policy is independently invalid.'],['Only the model loss matters.','Serving delay matters.']]},
{concept:'Semantic cache',prompt:'“Can I cancel order 123?” is reused for order 124 because vectors are close. Why is this unsafe?',answer:3,options:[['All similarity metrics are useless.','They help retrieval, not automatic identity equivalence.'],['Order IDs never matter.','They define the action target.'],['Temperature should be increased.','It does not fix cache identity.'],['Similar intent does not preserve entity, state or authorization.','Semantic reuse needs task-specific constraints.']]},
{concept:'Revocation',prompt:'Access was revoked after a private answer was cached. What should happen on the next cache lookup?',answer:0,options:[['Recheck authorization and deny/invalidate as appropriate.','Cached content remains protected.'],['Return it until TTL expires regardless.','That extends revoked access.'],['Publish it globally.','That increases exposure.'],['Trust the old prompt alone.','Prompts are not access controls.']]},
{concept:'Stampede',prompt:'A popular cache key expires and 1,000 requests regenerate it simultaneously. Which control helps?',answer:2,options:[['Identical synchronized expiries for all keys.','That amplifies bursts.'],['Unlimited backend calls.','That overloads the source.'],['Single-flight coalescing and staggered expiry.','They reduce duplicate regeneration.'],['Delete the registry.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Cache measurement',prompt:'Hit rate is 95% but answers cite obsolete policies. What should be optimized?',answer:1,options:[['Hit rate alone.','It rewards stale responses.'],['Correct, authorized, fresh task completion and cost.','Usefulness constrains efficiency.'],['Only response byte count.','It misses meaning.'],['Longer TTL without checks.','That extends staleness.']]}
]});
register({id:58,centralQuestion:'At what concurrency does additional work stop increasing throughput and start extending everyone’s wait?',objective:'Use Little’s Law, utilization, realistic load generation and latency decomposition to find the saturation knee and set safe concurrency.',sections:[
{id:'law',title:'In-flight work connects arrival rate and residence time',body:['For a stable system, Little’s Law states L=λW: average work in the system equals average arrival/completion rate multiplied by average residence time. At 200 requests/s and 0.25 s average end-to-end residence, average in-flight work is 50. The law uses consistent boundaries and averages; it does not directly predict p99 or justify an unstable infinite queue.','Concurrency includes active execution and waiting. Increasing it can keep devices busy when one request leaves resources idle. Once the bottleneck is saturated, extra concurrency mainly adds waiting and memory pressure. The knee is the region where latency grows sharply while useful throughput gains diminish.','Utilization near 100% leaves little ability to absorb variability. Real requests have different sizes and dependencies, and arrivals are bursty. Capacity planning needs headroom chosen from the SLO and arrival distribution rather than a universal utilization percentage.'],table:table('Load-test interpretation',['Observation','Likely meaning','Next measurement'],[['Throughput rises, latency stable','Unused parallel capacity','Continue controlled sweep'],['Throughput flat, queue rises','Saturation','Locate bottleneck and admission limit'],['Errors rise, latency falls','Fast failures or shedding','Useful success and error classes'],['p99 rises only on long inputs','Shape-dependent contention','Length slices and memory'],['Warm test passes, cold fails','Startup/cache dependency','Cold path and scale-out time']]),check:{question:'At 80 QPS and 0.5 seconds average residence, what is average in-flight work?',answer:'40, assuming the measured system is stable and the boundaries match.'}},
{id:'load',title:'The load generator can hide the failure',body:['A closed-loop client sends a request and waits before sending the next. When the server slows, the client also slows its offered load. This can hide queueing a real open arrival process would create, sometimes called coordinated omission in latency measurement. Use an arrival model that represents actual demand and records intended send time as well as completion.','Test a matrix of request lengths, concurrency, batch windows and replica counts. Include cold starts, cache misses, cancellation, one-replica loss and dependency tails. Measure received, accepted, completed and successful work separately; retries should not inflate useful throughput.','Capture histograms with suitable resolution, counts and time windows. Segment by route and input class without exploding metric cardinality. A single p99 across dissimilar endpoints can hide the interactive route behind a high-volume batch API.'],failure:'A benchmark that reports 1,000 QPS by counting failed retries measures traffic generation, not useful serving capacity. Define successful task completion before comparing results.',check:{question:'Why might a one-request-at-a-time benchmark understate overload latency?',answer:'It reduces offered load whenever the server slows, so it never creates the backlog real independent arrivals can produce.'}},
{id:'limits',title:'Translate the measured knee into an operating policy',body:['Set per-replica concurrency below the region where tail latency violates the objective, then reserve fleet capacity for failure and bursts. A queue limit should follow the remaining deadline and expected service time; it is not an arbitrary large integer. Rejecting excess work can preserve successful service for admitted work.','Long and short requests may need separate pools or scheduling classes. Otherwise long generations occupy memory and block short predictions. Preemption or cancellation must actually release resource state, not only close the client connection. Measure whether abandoned work continues using GPU time.','Optimization changes the knee. Quantization, a new model, different prompt lengths or a cache can alter throughput and memory. Bind capacity tests to the release manifest and rerun when a meaningful workload component changes.'],example:'Concurrency 8 gives 70 QPS at p99 300 ms; 16 gives 90 QPS at 500 ms; 32 gives 92 QPS at 2.5 s. Under a 600 ms p99 objective, 16 is a candidate ceiling, with additional headroom and failure testing before production.',check:{question:'Why not choose concurrency 32 merely because 92 QPS is the maximum?',answer:'The extra 2 QPS costs a severe SLO violation; capacity is constrained by acceptable latency and errors.'}}
],glossary:[['Little’s Law','Stable-system relation L=λW for average work, rate and residence.'],['Saturation knee','Region where extra load adds delay with little throughput gain.'],['Open-loop load','Arrivals generated independently of response completion.'],['Closed-loop load','New work generated in response to completion of prior work.'],['Coordinated omission','Latency undermeasurement when the generator omits arrivals during stalls.'],['Useful throughput','Rate of completed tasks meeting the success contract.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Find a defensible concurrency limit',brief:'At concurrency 4/8/16/32, throughput is 40/70/90/92 QPS and p99 is 180/300/500/2500 ms. Target is 600 ms.',parts:['Identify the knee and candidate operating limit.','Apply Little’s Law to 90 QPS at 0.2 s mean residence.','Design an open-arrival and long-input test.','Set cancellation, queue and N−1 capacity checks.'],solution:'The knee lies around 16; 32 adds negligible throughput and breaks p99. Average in-flight work at the stated mean is 18. Test realistic arrivals, bursts, long inputs and one-replica loss; 16 is provisional until these pass. Bound queue residence by remaining deadline, verify canceled work releases state, and size fleet from sustainable useful throughput.'},sources:{core:['azureWellArchitectedAI','otel'],deep:['kubernetesHPA']},quiz:[
{concept:'Little’s Law',prompt:'A stable API handles 150 QPS with mean residence 0.4 s. Average in-flight work is?',answer:0,options:[['60.','150 × 0.4.'],['375.','That divides instead of multiplying.'],['0.0027.','The units are inverted.'],['150 regardless of latency.','Residence time matters.']]},
{concept:'Saturation knee',prompt:'Concurrency rises 2×; throughput gains 2% and p99 rises 5×. What does this suggest?',answer:1,options:[['Unlimited spare capacity.','The data suggests saturation.'],['Extra work is mostly queueing or contending.','The bottleneck no longer benefits.'],['Better probability calibration.','No quality evidence is given.'],['A smaller test set is required.','This is load behavior.']]},
{concept:'Load generation',prompt:'A client waits for each response before sending another, even during stalls. What risk affects the test?',answer:2,options:[['It necessarily exceeds real demand.','It often reduces demand during stalls.'],['All latency is overstated.','Not necessarily.'],['It may hide independent-arrival backlog and coordinated omission.','Offered load slows with the service.'],['It proves p99 under arbitrary bursts.','It does not generate those bursts.']]},
{concept:'Useful throughput',prompt:'QPS doubles because every request is retried once, but successful tasks are unchanged. What improved?',answer:3,options:[['Useful throughput doubled.','Tasks did not increase.'],['Reliability doubled.','Retries may signal failures.'],['Cost efficiency doubled.','More work for the same output worsens it.'],['Only measured request traffic increased.','Count useful outcomes separately.']]},
{concept:'Cancellation',prompt:'Clients disconnect but GPU generation continues. What must be verified?',answer:0,options:[['Cancellation propagation and release of compute/KV state.','Otherwise abandoned work consumes capacity.'],['Only browser styling.','Unrelated.'],['Only model registration.','It does not stop active work.'],['No cancellation is ever needed.','Expired work can cause overload.']]},
{concept:'Capacity regression',prompt:'A prompt update doubles input length. Can the old capacity benchmark be assumed valid?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, the model weights are unchanged.','Prefill and memory demand changed.'],['No; remeasure the changed workload envelope.','Capacity depends on input shape.'],['Yes, if title text is unchanged.','Titles have no capacity meaning.'],['No prompt can be versioned.','Prompts should be versioned.']]},
{concept:'Latency objective',prompt:'Configuration A gives 90 QPS at p99 500 ms; B gives 92 QPS at 2.5 s. SLO is 600 ms. Which is a plausible base?',answer:2,options:[['B because throughput is highest.','It violates the SLO.'],['Either, because averages are absent.','The tail target is explicit.'],['A, followed by headroom and failure tests.','It meets the stated tail constraint.'],['Neither can be measured.','The supplied measurements are usable.']]}
]});
register({id:59,centralQuestion:'Which shared dependency can still bring down every replica at once?',objective:'Identify single points of failure and correlated failure domains; combine health checks, redundancy, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers and graceful degradation.',sections:[
{id:'redundancy',title:'Replica count is not independent redundancy',body:['High availability keeps a service accessible despite expected failures. Redundancy adds alternate capacity; fault tolerance defines how the system continues when components fail. Three API replicas sharing one node, database, credential, DNS path or region may still fail together. Draw the entire dependency graph, not just the serving tier.','A single point of failure is any component whose loss alone breaks the promised service. Removing it may require replication, failover, alternate paths or a reduced service mode. Each extra component can also add complexity and new failure combinations, so reliability work must follow the actual service objective.','Independent failure domains matter: separate processes protect against a process crash, nodes against node loss, zones against zone failure, and regions against broader outages. Cross-region operation introduces consistency, latency, data-location and operational costs. Choose the scope that the business risk justifies.'],table:table('Failure control',['Control','Protects against','Can still fail when'],[['Replicas','Instance loss','Shared node/dependency fails'],['Readiness','Routing to unready instance','Probe ignores model compatibility'],['Liveness','Internal stuck process','Restart storm follows external outage'],['Timeout','Unbounded waiting','Retries multiply beyond deadline'],['Circuit breaker','Repeated dependency pressure','Fallback is untested'],['Backup','Historical data loss','Restore is too slow or broken']]),check:{question:'Why can a single shared secret become an availability risk?',answer:'Revocation, expiry or misconfiguration can simultaneously disable every replica using it.'}},
{id:'resilience',title:'Bound failures before they cascade',body:['Timeouts bound how long a caller occupies resources. Propagate a total deadline so nested calls do not each consume the full original budget. Retry only transient, safe-to-repeat failures, with capped attempts and jittered backoff. Idempotency is essential for side effects.','A circuit breaker stops calling a dependency after a failure threshold, waits, then permits limited probes to test recovery. It avoids spending every request on a known failing path. Thresholds and reset behavior need tuning; a breaker that opens on one harmless spike can reduce availability unnecessarily.','Bulkheads isolate pools so one dependency or tenant cannot consume all threads, connections or GPU slots. Graceful degradation provides a smaller validated promise: cached public answers, simpler recommendations, delayed batch completion or human review. Returning a plausible unsupported answer is not graceful degradation in a high-risk workflow.'],failure:'Retrying at the client, gateway and model service three times each can produce up to 27 downstream attempts for one logical request. Place a shared attempt/deadline budget across the call tree.',check:{question:'What is the purpose of a half-open circuit-breaker state?',answer:'It admits a small number of probes to determine whether the dependency recovered without releasing the full backlog.'}},
{id:'verify',title:'Health checks and drills must represent useful recovery',body:['Startup checks allow legitimate model loading; readiness checks determine whether new traffic can be accepted; liveness checks detect a process that cannot recover without restart. A TCP port test is too weak to establish model compatibility. A probe that runs expensive full inference every second can itself consume meaningful capacity.','Test failure injection at controlled scope: kill one replica, block a dependency, expire a credential in a test environment, slow artifact storage and restore a backup. Observe detection, failover, queue behavior, data consistency and user-path success. A failover that takes ten minutes does not meet a one-minute recovery objective.','Keep runbooks, ownership and escalation current. Incident communication should distinguish containment, partial restoration and verified recovery. The architecture should make a safe reduced mode explicit before the emergency, including when to abstain rather than continue.'],example:'A RAG store is unavailable. A customer-policy assistant can return a clear temporary-unavailability response or approved static guidance. It should not improvise policy from model memory while displaying normal citations.',check:{question:'Why must a fallback be evaluated independently?',answer:'Its data freshness, quality, permissions and user consequences differ from the primary path; it can introduce a new unsafe failure mode.'}}
],glossary:[['Single point of failure','Component whose loss alone breaks the service promise.'],['Bulkhead','Resource isolation preventing one failure from exhausting unrelated work.'],['Circuit breaker','Control that pauses repeated calls to a failing dependency and probes recovery.'],['Graceful degradation','Validated reduction in capability during failure.'],['Fault tolerance','Ability to continue specified operation despite component failures.'],['Correlated failure','Multiple components failing from a shared cause.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Find hidden common-mode failures',brief:'A three-zone API depends on one vector store, one identity service and a shared secret; retries exist at three layers.',parts:['Map single points and correlated risks.','Allocate end-to-end deadlines and retry budgets.','Design breaker/bulkhead/fallback policies.','Write a controlled failure drill and recovery criteria.'],solution:'The vector store, identity path and secret are shared risks despite zone spread. Replicate or provide validated alternatives where justified; never bypass authorization when identity is unavailable. Share deadline/attempt budgets, isolate pools, and open breakers on sustained dependency failure. Test store slowdown, credential rotation and one-zone loss; verify useful success, p99, permissions and recovery time.'},sources:{core:['kubernetesProbes','azureWellArchitectedAI'],deep:['otelContext']},quiz:[
{concept:'Single point of failure',prompt:'Six API replicas share one unreplicated vector store. What remains?',answer:1,options:[['No possible failure.','The shared store can fail.'],['A shared dependency that can stop the whole RAG path.','Replica count does not remove it.'],['Only a training concern.','It is a serving dependency.'],['Guaranteed cross-region resilience.','No such design is given.']]},
{concept:'Retry multiplication',prompt:'Three layers each attempt a call three times. Worst-case downstream attempts can reach?',answer:0,options:[['27.','3 × 3 × 3.'],['3 always.','Independent layers multiply.'],['0.','Calls still occur.'],['6.','Adding attempts understates nesting.']]},
{concept:'Circuit breaker',prompt:'A dependency is consistently failing. Why open a breaker?',answer:2,options:[['To guarantee the dependency is repaired.','The breaker only controls calls.'],['To bypass authorization.','That would be unsafe.'],['To limit wasted calls and pressure while testing recovery cautiously.','It contains cascading demand.'],['To delete all queued evidence.','Evidence should be retained.']]},
{concept:'Fallback',prompt:'Policy retrieval fails. The assistant invents an answer from memory with fabricated citations. Is this graceful degradation?',answer:3,options:[['Yes, any answer preserves availability.','It violates the correctness promise.'],['Yes, if fluent.','Fluency is not evidence.'],['Yes, if cheaper.','Cost does not justify fabrication.'],['No; use a validated limited response or abstention.','Reduced service must remain trustworthy.']]},
{concept:'Probe semantics',prompt:'The process is alive but the model is still loading. Which signal should exclude traffic?',answer:0,options:[['Readiness/startup status.','Traffic should wait for usable model state.'],['Training accuracy alone.','It is not current readiness.'],['The number of Services.','Count does not establish health.'],['A forced liveness restart every second.','That can prevent loading entirely.']]},
{concept:'Bulkhead',prompt:'A slow external tool exhausts every worker thread, blocking unrelated predictions. Which design helps?',answer:1,options:[['One larger shared unlimited pool.','It preserves coupling.'],['Separate bounded resource pools for the tool and core inference.','The failure cannot consume all capacity.'],['Remove deadlines.','That holds threads longer.'],['Retry more aggressively.','That amplifies contention.']]},
{concept:'Recovery testing',prompt:'A backup exists but has never been restored. What is unknown?',answer:2,options:[['Whether the file has a name.','That is trivial.'],['Whether all model metrics improve.','Backups do not ensure quality.'],['Whether recovery works and meets RPO/RTO with compatible dependencies.','Only restore evidence establishes this.'],['Whether HTTP supports GET.','Unrelated.']]}
]});
register({id:60,centralQuestion:'What capacity and recovery promise can the organization afford to sustain through peak demand and failure?',objective:'Build a capacity and cost model using realistic workload units, N−1 capacity, headroom, utilization, lifecycle cost and sensitivity analysis.',sections:[
{id:'demand',title:'Forecast work, not only user count',body:['Translate business demand into requests, input sizes, output tokens, batch jobs, storage growth and dependency calls. Ten thousand users with short classifications differ from ten thousand users generating long reports. Include concurrency, arrival bursts, retry rates, cache hit/miss mix and seasonal peaks. State uncertainty ranges rather than presenting one precise-looking forecast.','Benchmark per-replica sustainable throughput at the quality and latency objective. Then account for one or more failed units according to the resilience promise. If demand is 300 QPS and each replica safely handles 80 QPS, four meet normal load but five are needed to retain at least 320 QPS after one loss. Routing and shared-store limits may require more.','Capacity includes startup and recovery. A fleet that takes 15 minutes to provision cannot serve an unpredicted one-minute spike without warm reserve, demand shaping or degradation. Artifact bandwidth, IP limits, quotas and database connections can cap scale before compute capacity does.'],check:{question:'Why is user count an insufficient sizing unit?',answer:'Users generate different rates, lengths, tool calls and concurrency; resource demand follows work shape and arrival timing.'}},
{id:'cost',title:'Total cost includes idle capacity and human work',body:['Compute cost includes active and idle resources, reserved minimums, training, evaluation, retries and failed experiments. Add storage, network egress, vector databases, observability, backups, security controls, engineering, on-call and incident remediation. A vendor token price is one term in the total cost of ownership.','GPU utilization should be interpreted with successful work. High utilization from padding, duplicate retries or discarded generation is waste. Cost per successful task connects infrastructure to value. Track unit costs by workload and release; averages can hide a costly long-tail route or tenant.','Compare alternatives over the same service promise. A cheaper single-zone design is not equivalent to a multi-zone N−1 design. A smaller model may save cost and improve latency while reducing task quality; quantify whether that loss crosses the business floor. Use sensitivity analysis on traffic, context length, cache hit rate and staffing assumptions.'],table:table('Capacity decision record',['Input','Example','Why it matters'],[['Peak useful demand','300 QPS','Defines load to serve'],['Per-replica SLO capacity','80 QPS','Uses acceptable performance'],['Failure reserve','One replica','Changes minimum fleet'],['Warmup','5 minutes','Limits reactive elasticity'],['Unit economics','Cost/successful task','Includes failed/idle work'],['Uncertainty','±40% demand','Tests budget robustness']]),failure:'Comparing only list price per GPU-hour can favor a device that finishes fewer useful tasks, needs more replicas, or fails the latency objective. Normalize by the same quality and reliability promise.',check:{question:'Why include on-call and incident work in TCO?',answer:'Operating complexity consumes recurring human capacity and can dominate savings from cheaper infrastructure.'}},
{id:'tradeoffs',title:'Reliability investment should follow consequence',body:['An internal exploratory report may tolerate delayed completion. A payment authorization service may require low latency and rapid failover. Choose redundancy, backup, region scope and human fallback from the cost of failure and recovery targets. Avoid both underprotection and expensive availability features without an identified business need.','Use an architecture decision record to state options, assumptions, measured evidence, selected design, rejected alternatives and revisit triggers. For example, begin with CPU serving while traffic is low, then reconsider GPU batching when demand crosses a measured utilization threshold. A decision with a trigger is more useful than a permanent claim that one platform is best.','Capacity review is continuous. New prompts, model versions, data growth and adoption can change the envelope. Monitor actual versus forecast, budget burn and saturation. Test the chosen failure scenario before committing to a service promise.'],example:'A monthly report system can use queued batch jobs and interruptible capacity with checkpoints. A 300 ms emergency-screening API needs warm reliable capacity and a safe escalation workflow. Applying the same fleet policy to both wastes money or creates unacceptable risk.',check:{question:'What belongs in a revisit trigger?',answer:'A measurable condition such as sustained traffic, unit cost, p99, model memory, data growth or recovery requirements that invalidates a key assumption.'}},
{id:'integrated',title:'Make the recommendation falsifiable',body:['A defensible recommendation names the workload envelope, release, measured benchmark, fleet size, failure reserve, overload response, monthly cost range and validation plan. It also states what is excluded: for example, a region-wide outage may be outside the current SLO and require an approved recovery plan rather than instant failover.','Compare at least two architectures on the same demand scenarios. Include low utilization, expected load, peak and failure. A configuration that is cheapest at steady peak may be expensive at the actual daily duty cycle. Simulate cache loss and long-context growth because both can multiply backend demand.','The final engineering judgment combines D1 evaluation quality, D4 release/reliability controls, D5 resource behavior, D6 risk and D7 value. Infrastructure does not make a weak model useful, and a strong model does not excuse an unserviceable architecture.'],check:{question:'What makes a capacity claim reviewable?',answer:'Explicit assumptions and workload, measured SLO throughput, failure scenario, cost model and a test that could disprove the claim.'}}
],glossary:[['Capacity envelope','Workload range a system can serve within its objectives.'],['TCO','Total cost of ownership across build, operation, risk and retirement.'],['Unit economics','Cost and value per useful unit of work.'],['Sensitivity analysis','Testing how conclusions change when assumptions vary.'],['N−1 capacity','Capacity remaining after one specified unit fails.'],['Architecture decision record','Documented context, options, decision, consequences and revisit conditions.']],exercise:{duration:90,title:'Defend a production capacity proposal',brief:'Peak is 300 QPS, each replica handles 80 QPS at SLO, warmup is five minutes, and demand may grow 40%. Compare CPU and GPU designs with invented clearly labeled cost assumptions.',parts:['Calculate normal and N−1 fleet sizes for current and growth demand.','Build a monthly TCO sheet including idle reserve and supporting services.','Stress cache failure, long inputs and one-node loss.','Write a decision record with revisit triggers and an overload policy.','Take the D5 domain exam and review every uncertain answer.'],solution:'At 300 QPS, five replicas retain 320 QPS after one loss; at 420 QPS, seven retain 480 QPS. These are arithmetic lower bounds before routing/dependency headroom. Price the same SLO for each option, include idle/ops/storage/telemetry costs, and vary demand/context/cache assumptions. Maintain warm reserve or a bounded fallback during five-minute startup. Select from measured unit cost and risk, then test N−1 and shared dependency capacity.'},sources:{core:['azureWellArchitectedAI','kubernetesHPA'],deep:['otel','pytorchOptim']},quiz:[
{concept:'Capacity sizing',prompt:'Demand is 300 QPS; each replica serves 80 QPS at SLO. Minimum replicas for one-replica loss?',answer:0,options:[['Five.','Four survivors provide 320 QPS.'],['Four.','Three survivors provide only 240.'],['Three.','Even healthy capacity is insufficient.'],['One.','Far below demand.']]},
{concept:'Growth sizing',prompt:'Demand grows to 420 QPS with the same 80 QPS capacity. N−1 minimum becomes?',answer:2,options:[['Five.','Four survivors provide 320.'],['Six.','Five survivors provide 400.'],['Seven.','Six survivors provide 480.'],['Four.','Insufficient even without loss.']]},
{concept:'TCO',prompt:'A vendor quote covers tokens but omits integration, evaluation and on-call. What is it?',answer:1,options:[['Complete TCO.','Major lifecycle costs are absent.'],['One component of TCO.','Ownership includes human and supporting-system costs.'],['Guaranteed ROI.','Benefits are not quantified.'],['A reliability proof.','Pricing is not failure evidence.']]},
{concept:'Unit economics',prompt:'Device A costs less per hour but half as many tasks succeed. Which comparison matters?',answer:3,options:[['Hourly price alone.','It ignores useful output.'],['Parameter count alone.','It is not value.'],['Only maximum utilization.','Busy work can be waste.'],['Cost per successful task at the same SLO and quality.','It normalizes the service delivered.']]},
{concept:'Sensitivity',prompt:'A proposal is cheap only if cache hit rate stays 95%. What must be analyzed?',answer:0,options:[['Cost and capacity at lower hit rates and cache outage.','The key assumption may fail.'],['Only the cache logo.','Irrelevant.'],['No further scenario because the average is high.','Averages hide risk.'],['More training epochs automatically.','That does not restore cache hits.']]},
{concept:'Warm capacity',prompt:'Provisioning takes 15 minutes but spikes arrive in one minute. What is required?',answer:1,options:[['Assume instant scaling in the budget.','It contradicts startup evidence.'],['Warm reserve, demand shaping or a validated degraded mode.','Capacity must cover the delay.'],['Remove all limits.','Overload would spread.'],['Only more dashboards.','Observation does not create capacity.']]},
{concept:'Reliability comparison',prompt:'A single-zone option is cheaper than a multi-zone option. Can price alone decide?',answer:2,options:[['Yes, both provide identical failure coverage.','They do not.'],['Yes, outages never occur.','That is untenable.'],['No; compare against the same required failure and recovery promise.','Different risk coverage is not equivalent service.'],['No cost should ever matter.','Cost is part of the trade-off.']]},
{concept:'Decision record',prompt:'Which item makes an architecture decision adaptable?',answer:3,options:[['A claim it is best forever.','Assumptions change.'],['No rejected alternatives.','Reviewers need trade-offs.'],['No measurements.','That weakens evidence.'],['Explicit assumptions and measurable revisit triggers.','They identify when the decision must change.']]},
{concept:'Utilization',prompt:'GPU utilization rises because retries and padding doubled. Did efficiency necessarily improve?',answer:0,options:[['No; measure useful output and cost, not busy time alone.','The extra work may be waste.'],['Yes, all utilization is value.','This ignores failed and padded work.'],['Yes, accuracy must rise.','No quality evidence exists.'],['No GPU should be used.','The workload may still benefit after repair.']]},
{concept:'Capacity evidence',prompt:'A team promises p99 300 ms from a single warm request benchmark. What is missing?',answer:1,options:[['Only a longer title.','Irrelevant.'],['Realistic arrivals, concurrency, lengths, cold/failure paths and fleet tests.','One request does not establish service capacity.'],['An official passing score.','Unrelated and not supplied.'],['A lower loss value only.','Model fit does not establish serving latency.']]}
]});
})();
